# What is an AI-oriented programming language?

**The question.** Anticipate a practical answer to "What is an AI-oriented
programming language?", or define one. This is the first of the five questions
in the project README.

## Where the answer stands

The essay that opens the project collects the answers people give and finds
that they answer different questions.

| Answer | What it really answers | What it leaves out |
|---|---|---|
| The language models already know | Which language today's models write best | What a language designed for the purpose would be; popularity feeds training data, which feeds popularity |
| Rust, Lean, a verifier | How well the environment catches a model's mistakes | Whether the requirement was right: proving a program meets a specification is not proving the specification is what was wanted |
| Laws and proofs beside agent instructions | Which instructions can become checkable contracts | The instructions that cannot be formalized |
| Syntax trees in place of source text | What interface a machine should write to | Models are trained on text |
| English | How to express an intention | Saying something does not make it executable, consistent or verifiable |

The essay then sets aside a tempting summary, that programs solve reducible
problems and language models irreducible ones. A model is itself a
computation and escapes no limit of computation. Irreducible, undecidable and
underspecified are three different conditions.

What survives is a different line: **executing an established formalization
versus discovering or revising the formalization itself**. A conventional
language serves the first. A language model is useful for the second. In the
terms of this vault the first is the deductive arc of the reasoning cycle and
the second the inductive arc, and neither works alone.

So the proposed answer is that an AI-oriented language is not a syntax that
models find easy to emit. It is an environment in which formal computation and
informal reasoning interact continuously: specifications, contracts, code,
observations and generated knowledge are all inspectable and executable
artifacts, an agent can run a program, inspect the result and revise its own
description of the problem, and a person can inspect, interrupt and redirect.
The essay's closing sentence states the aim: systems in which knowledge
becomes code, code produces knowledge, and both take part in revising their
own representations.

That last clause is where this question meets the one about strange loops.

